{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The problem does not contain a sure outcome (i.e., no 100% probability outcome), so the reference value is zero, as specified. The reason evaluates each lottery by computing the difference between each outcome and the reference value (zero), then considers the mean (expected) value of these differences, weighted by their probabilities. For Lottery A: outcomes are -4000 (25%), -2000 (25%), and 0 (50%). Differences from reference (0) are -4000, -2000, and 0, so the mean difference is: (0.25 × -4000) + (0.25 × -2000) + (0.5 × 0) = -1000 - 500 + 0 = -1500. For Lottery B: outcomes are -6000 (25%) and 0 (75%). Differences are -6000 and 0, so mean difference is: (0.25 × -6000) + (0.75 × 0) = -1500. Both lotteries have the same mean difference (-1500), so the decision reason would be indifferent between them.",
